Title: gaze_close records the next line's key as the seats claim when seats: is empty
Created: 2026-11-01
Author: reviewer

--- log ---
2026-11-01T12:59Z reviewer created

--- body ---
## Context
Found by raid 1f0534 while reading incident 1411 (arena, answer 20261031T230954325Z-padme-5f5af213).
`scripts/gaze_close.py` `seats_claims` matches `^[\s>#-]*seats\s*:\s*([A-Za-z][\w-]*)` on text whose
`*_\`` marks were stripped first. `\s*` after the colon crosses the newline and the stripping removed
the underscores of the next key, so an empty `seats:` line followed by `reviewed_head_sha: …` records
`reviewedheadsha` as the seats claim in refs/notes/review. Reproduced on main 2a5547b8:
`seats_claim('result: REVIEWED\nseats:\nreviewed_head_sha: 7fdb24d\n') == 'reviewedheadsha'`.

## Scope
The value must sit on the key's own line; an empty `seats:` yields no claim (the caller's `self-only`
fallback then applies). Check `field()` (same file, `^{name}:\s*(\S+)`) for the same newline crossing.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] A test: an empty `seats:` line followed by another key yields no claim
- [ ] Existing seats-claim tests (list, quote, heading marks, markup around the key) still pass

## Imagine (raid 1f0534)
- Fix is one regex: `[ \t]*` instead of `\s*` around the colon, so the value stays on the key's line. Bounded; cheap worker.
